
  Worth noting: because identity is a local picker, you can switch users in one browser. I'd have the store sync across
  tabs (BroadcastChannel), which makes "another user changed your task" actually demonstrable — and is the same shape a
  server push would take later.

[38;5;246m⏺ [39mUser answered Claude's questions:
[38;5;246m  ⎿  · Where should notifications surface? → Inbox + toasts (Recommended)[39m

[38;5;231m⏺[39m Next: which changes are worth a notification. Over-notifying is the usual way these systems die.

  - [1mHigh-signal only[0m — status changes (e.g. done/reopened) and assignment changes. Almost never noise, but a silently
    changed due date is invisible.
  - [1mAll meaningful field changes[0m — status, assignment, title, due date, plus deletion. Catches the cases people actually
    get burned by. Risk is chatter on heavily-edited tasks.
  - [1mEverything, filtered in UI[0m — record every change and let users filter. Most flexible, but it pushes the curation
    problem onto the user and makes the unread count useless by default.

  I recommend the middle one, with two rules baked in: you're never notified about your own actions, and rapid repeated
  edits to the same task by the same person collapse into one notification. Those two rules do most of the noise control
  the third option tries to buy with UI, and they're cheap when designed in from the start rather than bolted on.

  Description edits are the borderline case — I'd leave them out, since they're the most frequently churned field and
  the least likely to matter to a watcher.
